<article>

# Processing an access request # {#h-eval}

<section>

## Completing the permission ticket ## {#h-eval-prep}

<!-- Authorization assessment (Section 3.3.4) involves the authorization server assembling and evaluating policy conditions, scopes, claims, and any other relevant information. -->

Upon receiving an [=access request=], the [=[AS]=] [SHOULD] process it according to the following algorithm.
  
  ISSUE: Go into the combination of `permissions` and `scope` (and possibly `ticket`).
 
  1. If the [=access request=] contains a `ticket` parameter, the [=[AS]=] looks up the corresponding [=ticket=] in its knowledge base.
  
    - If the value of the `ticket` parameter is a valid ticket identifier, the corresponding [=ticket=] is used as target of the request.

    - If no [=ticket=] with the corresponding identifier is found, the [=[AS]=] [SHOULD] respond with error code `invalid_grant` (cf. [[!UMA-Fed]], [[UMA-Fed#rfc.section.6|§ 6]]).
 
  2. If the [=access request=] contains a `permissions` parameter, the [=[AS]=] [SHOULD] create a new [=ticket=], following the steps described in [[#h-fed-res-ticket]]. This [=ticket=] is then used as target of the request.

  3. If the [=access request=] contains a `scope` parameter:

     1. <strong>Look up the metadata of the [=client=]</strong>, in particular the list of [=scopes=] for which it is pre-registered. 
    <!--RegisteredScopes-->

     2. Split the string value of the `scope` parameter on space characters.
    <!--RequestedScopes-->
    
     3. For each element of the split string:
    
      - If the string is a [=scope identifier=] present in the list of pre-registered [=scopes=] of the [=client=]: <strong>add the [=scope=]</strong> to the list of requested [=scopes=] for each of the [=permission descriptions=] in the target [=ticket=].

        NOTE: the UMA algorithm keeps these separate, possibly allowing the extra scopes to vary between different messages in the authorization flow/negotiation... again, this seems to parallel the permissions object.

      - If not, the [=[AS]=] [MAY] **disregard** the element, or send an error response with error code `invalid_scope` (cf. [[!UMA-Fed]], [[UMA-Fed#rfc.section.6|§ 6]]).

</section>
<section>

## Determining the policy decision ## {#h-eval-engine}

<!-- Authorization results determination (Section 3.3.4) -->

After having completed the information in the permission ticket, it is time for the [=[AS]=] to determine the authorization decision as a result of the [=[RO]'s=] policies, applied to the available information.

Just like [UMA], the [A4DS] profile does not specify the mechanism by which this decision is to be made. On top of [UMA], however, this specification adds an interface through which an [=[AS]=] [MAY] communicate with any interoperable [=policy engine=], thereby clearly separating the domain of exchange and negotiation [DASH] between the [=client=] and the [=[AS]=] [DASH] from the domain of decision-making.

[A4DS] therefore defines a <dfn export>policy engine</dfn> as any (implementation of an) algorithm that, given a complete permission ticket [DASH] including all requested permissions and all currently provided claims [DASH] can determine all active/operative policy conditions (and other relevant information, serving as state of the world), and evaluate their authorization status (i.e., compliance).

</section>
<section>

## Granting an access token ## {#h-eval-token}

<!-- The authorization server returns:
  - a success code, an RPT, and an optional PCT (Section 3.3.5); or
  - an error code (Section 3.3.6), and a ticket in case of need_info or request_submitted. -->

<!-- For each scope in RequestedScopes(resource) that passes the evaluation, add it to a set called CandidateGrantedScopes(resource). -->

When the [=[AS]=] concludes that the provided claims are sufficient to grant the [=client=] access to the requested resources, it responds with an [=access token=] and `Authorization` scheme, as specified in [[!UMA-Grant]], [[UMA-Grant#uma-grant-type|§ 3.3.5]] (see also [[!RFC9110]], [[RFC9110#field.authorization|§ 11.6.2]])). 
 
For example:

  ```http
  HTTP/1.1 200 OK
  Content-Type: application/json
  ... 

  { 
    "access_token": "sbjsbhs(/SSJHBSUSSJHVhjsgvhsgvshgsv",
    "token_type": "Bearer"
  }
  ```

The [=client=] can then use this [=access token=] to access the required resources at the [=[RS]=] (cf. [[#h-access-token]]).

</section>
</article>
